description:
Currently, if a workflow has a fitted preprocessor, `outcome_names()` does not do the right thing since it checks to see if the _model_ has been fit. 

```r
outcome_names.workflow <- function(x, ...) {
  if (!is.null(x$fit$fit)) {   #<- This here is wrong
    y_vals <- extract_mold(x)$outcomes
    res <- colnames(y_vals)
  } else {
    preprocessor <- extract_preprocessor(x)
    res <- outcome_names(preprocessor, ...)
  }
  res
}
```

Instead, it should emulate `workflows:::has_mold()` and instead check that `!is.null(x$pre$mold)`. 

This doesn't check the difference in some cases, such as #1024

`show_best()` and others have code that looks like: 

```r
  if (maximize) {
    summary_res <- summary_res %>% dplyr::arrange(dplyr::desc(mean))
  } else {
    summary_res <- summary_res %>% dplyr::arrange(mean)
  }
```

This should be adjusted for cases when there is a target value. 

Perhaps we can use `maximize = FALSE` and arrange by `(abs(mean - target))`. 

@DavisVaughan
